<main id="main" class="page-head">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow rv">Ecology on the ground</p>
    <h1 class="rv page-title">13 species,<br>recorded on the trails.</h1>
    <p class="lede rv">Logged on the Corrin Loop and the Old Cattle Trail in August 2026, with a determination for each.</p>
    <p class="rv src-note">Records made using <a href="https://plantnet.org/">Pl@ntNet</a>. Descriptions are summarised from the <a href="https://plantatlas2020.org/">BSBI Plant Atlas 2020</a>, with the account for each species linked beneath it.</p>

    <ul class="splist rv">
      <li class="sp sp-nofig">
        <h2 class="sp-name">Autumn hawkbit</h2>
        <span class="sp-sci sp-none">Family <em>Asteraceae</em></span>
        <ul class="sp-recs">
          <li><span class="sp-trail">Corrin Loop</span><span class="sp-date"></span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li class="sp">
        <figure class="sp-fig"><picture><source type="image/avif" srcset="../assets/img/species/bilberry-380.avif 380w, ../assets/img/species/bilberry-760.avif 760w" sizes="(min-width:700px) 150px, 300px"><source type="image/webp" srcset="../assets/img/species/bilberry-380.webp 380w, ../assets/img/species/bilberry-760.webp 760w" sizes="(min-width:700px) 150px, 300px"><img src="../assets/img/species/bilberry-380.webp" width="380" height="253" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="A low bilberry shrub carrying ripe blue-black berries among small oval leaves, open hillside behind"></picture><figcaption><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vaccinium_myrtillus_L._6382.jpg">Lalakmus</a>, <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0">CC BY-SA 4.0</a></figcaption></figure>
        <h2 class="sp-name">Bilberry</h2>
        <span class="sp-sci"><em>Vaccinium myrtillus</em> L.</span>
        <p class="sp-desc">A low shrub of acid ground that will not grow over lime. It is often dominant alongside heather on well-drained heath and moor, particularly in the uplands, and forms an understorey in birch, pine and oak woodland. It rarely regenerates from seed.</p><p class="sp-cite"><a href="https://plantatlas2020.org/atlas/2cd4p9h.w50" rel="noopener">BSBI Plant Atlas 2020</a></p>
        <ul class="sp-recs">
          <li><span class="sp-trail">Corrin Loop</span><span class="sp-date">15 August 2026</span></li>
          <li><span class="sp-trail">Old Cattle Trail</span><span class="sp-date">15 August 2026</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li class="sp">
        <figure class="sp-fig"><picture><source type="image/avif" srcset="../assets/img/species/common-figwort-380.avif 380w, ../assets/img/species/common-figwort-760.avif 760w" sizes="(min-width:700px) 150px, 300px"><source type="image/webp" srcset="../assets/img/species/common-figwort-380.webp 380w, ../assets/img/species/common-figwort-760.webp 760w" sizes="(min-width:700px) 150px, 300px"><img src="../assets/img/species/common-figwort-380.webp" width="380" height="253" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="An upright stem carrying small maroon and green figwort flowers above opposite toothed leaves"></picture><figcaption><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scrophularia_nodosa_OM52.JPG">OldMuzzle</a>, <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0">CC BY-SA 3.0</a></figcaption></figure>
        <h2 class="sp-name">Common figwort</h2>
        <span class="sp-sci"><em>Scrophularia nodosa</em> L.</span>
        <p class="sp-desc">A perennial herb that takes either sun or shade and prefers a fertile soil. It grows in damp woodland and along woodland rides, hedgebanks, ditches and riversides, and turns up occasionally on drier waste ground. Mostly a lowland plant.</p><p class="sp-cite"><a href="https://plantatlas2020.org/atlas/2cd4p9h.ck1" rel="noopener">BSBI Plant Atlas 2020</a></p>
        <ul class="sp-recs">
          <li><span class="sp-trail">Old Cattle Trail</span><span class="sp-date">9 August 2026</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li class="sp">
        <figure class="sp-fig"><picture><source type="image/avif" srcset="../assets/img/species/devils-bit-scabious-380.avif 380w, ../assets/img/species/devils-bit-scabious-760.avif 760w" sizes="(min-width:700px) 150px, 300px"><source type="image/webp" srcset="../assets/img/species/devils-bit-scabious-380.webp 380w, ../assets/img/species/devils-bit-scabious-760.webp 760w" sizes="(min-width:700px) 150px, 300px"><img src="../assets/img/species/devils-bit-scabious-380.webp" width="380" height="253" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Two rounded flower heads on slender stems, one still in bud and one open to lilac florets"></picture><figcaption><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Succisa_pratensis_-_Niitv%C3%A4lja_bog_-_Keila.jpg">Ivar Leidus</a>, <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0">CC BY-SA 4.0</a></figcaption></figure>
        <h2 class="sp-name">Devil&#x27;s bit Scabious</h2>
        <span class="sp-sci"><em>Succisa pratensis</em> Moench</span>
        <p class="sp-desc">A perennial herb of damp to moderately free-draining ground, favouring mildly acid soils. It grows in woodland rides, on heath and in grassland, and in fens and mires; in the uplands it also holds on to cliff ledges and ravines.</p><p class="sp-cite"><a href="https://plantatlas2020.org/atlas/2cd4p9h.23w" rel="noopener">BSBI Plant Atlas 2020</a></p>
        <ul class="sp-recs">
          <li><span class="sp-trail">Corrin Loop</span><span class="sp-date">15 August 2026</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li class="sp">
        <figure class="sp-fig"><picture><source type="image/avif" srcset="../assets/img/species/grey-willow-380.avif 380w, ../assets/img/species/grey-willow-760.avif 760w" sizes="(min-width:700px) 150px, 300px"><source type="image/webp" srcset="../assets/img/species/grey-willow-380.webp 380w, ../assets/img/species/grey-willow-760.webp 760w" sizes="(min-width:700px) 150px, 300px"><img src="../assets/img/species/grey-willow-380.webp" width="380" height="253" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Male willow catkins dense with pale yellow stamens on bare spring twigs"></picture><figcaption><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salix_cinerea_flowers-2.jpg">BCB</a>, <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/">CC BY-SA 3.0</a></figcaption></figure>
        <h2 class="sp-name">Grey Willow</h2>
        <span class="sp-sci"><em>Salix cinerea</em> L.</span>
        <p class="sp-desc">A shrub or small tree of wet ground: wet woodland and carr, marshes and fens, reedbeds, bogs, and the banks of rivers and streams. It also colonises damp waste ground and old pits. Two subspecies occur in Britain and Ireland.</p><p class="sp-cite"><a href="https://plantatlas2020.org/atlas/2cd4p9h.1t0" rel="noopener">BSBI Plant Atlas 2020</a></p>
        <ul class="sp-recs">
          <li><span class="sp-trail">Corrin Loop</span><span class="sp-date">15 August 2026</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li class="sp">
        <figure class="sp-fig"><picture><source type="image/avif" srcset="../assets/img/species/heather-380.avif 380w, ../assets/img/species/heather-760.avif 760w" sizes="(min-width:700px) 150px, 300px"><source type="image/webp" srcset="../assets/img/species/heather-380.webp 380w, ../assets/img/species/heather-760.webp 760w" sizes="(min-width:700px) 150px, 300px"><img src="../assets/img/species/heather-380.webp" width="380" height="253" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="A spreading mat of heather in flower, fine needle-like leaves on wiry stems"></picture><figcaption><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Calluna_vulgaris_kz33.jpg">Krzysztof Ziarnek, Kenraiz</a>, <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0">CC BY 4.0</a></figcaption></figure>
        <h2 class="sp-name">Heather</h2>
        <span class="sp-sci"><em>Calluna vulgaris</em> (L.) Hull</span>
        <p class="sp-desc">A low, clump-forming shrub, often the dominant plant on heath, moor and open woodland over acid soils. It takes ground ranging from dry and exposed to wet blanket bog and raised mire. It can colonise newly opened ground such as clearfell in plantations, regenerating from seed long buried in the soil.</p><p class="sp-cite"><a href="https://plantatlas2020.org/atlas/2cd4p9h.yea" rel="noopener">BSBI Plant Atlas 2020</a></p>
        <ul class="sp-recs">
          <li><span class="sp-trail">Corrin Loop</span><span class="sp-date">15 August 2026</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li class="sp">
        <figure class="sp-fig"><picture><source type="image/avif" srcset="../assets/img/species/herb-robert-380.avif 380w, ../assets/img/species/herb-robert-760.avif 760w" sizes="(min-width:700px) 150px, 300px"><source type="image/webp" srcset="../assets/img/species/herb-robert-380.webp 380w, ../assets/img/species/herb-robert-760.webp 760w" sizes="(min-width:700px) 150px, 300px"><img src="../assets/img/species/herb-robert-380.webp" width="380" height="253" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="A five-petalled pink flower above deeply divided green leaves"></picture><figcaption><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Geranium_robertianum_04.2025_(3).jpg">DidierFy</a>, <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0">CC BY-SA 4.0</a></figcaption></figure>
        <h2 class="sp-name">Herb Robert</h2>
        <span class="sp-sci"><em>Geranium robertianum</em> L.</span>
        <p class="sp-desc">An annual or biennial herb that tolerates shade and grows on most soils except the strongly acid. Its habitats run from woodland, hedgerows and shaded banks to walls, scree and coastal shingle, and it is very common on waste ground and brickwork in towns.</p><p class="sp-cite"><a href="https://plantatlas2020.org/atlas/2cd4p9h.8nb" rel="noopener">BSBI Plant Atlas 2020</a></p>
        <ul class="sp-recs">
          <li><span class="sp-trail">Corrin Loop</span><span class="sp-date">15 August 2026</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li class="sp">
        <figure class="sp-fig"><picture><source type="image/avif" srcset="../assets/img/species/marsh-thistle-380.avif 380w, ../assets/img/species/marsh-thistle-760.avif 760w" sizes="(min-width:700px) 150px, 300px"><source type="image/webp" srcset="../assets/img/species/marsh-thistle-380.webp 380w, ../assets/img/species/marsh-thistle-760.webp 760w" sizes="(min-width:700px) 150px, 300px"><img src="../assets/img/species/marsh-thistle-380.webp" width="380" height="253" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="A spiny winged stem carrying clustered purple thistle heads"></picture><figcaption><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cirsium_palustre_06.2026_(2).jpg">DidierFy</a>, <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0">CC BY-SA 4.0</a></figcaption></figure>
        <h2 class="sp-name">Marsh thistle</h2>
        <span class="sp-sci"><em>Cirsium palustre</em> (L.) Scop.</span>
        <p class="sp-desc">A perennial herb that flowers once and then dies, found in mires, fens and marshes, damp grassland and rush pasture, wet woodland, and montane springs and flushes. It spreads only by seed, which can lie in the soil for many years before it germinates.</p><p class="sp-cite"><a href="https://plantatlas2020.org/atlas/2cd4p9h.8hn" rel="noopener">BSBI Plant Atlas 2020</a></p>
        <ul class="sp-recs">
          <li><span class="sp-trail">Corrin Loop</span><span class="sp-date">15 August 2026</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li class="sp">
        <figure class="sp-fig"><picture><source type="image/avif" srcset="../assets/img/species/purple-moor-grass-380.avif 380w, ../assets/img/species/purple-moor-grass-760.avif 760w" sizes="(min-width:700px) 150px, 300px"><source type="image/webp" srcset="../assets/img/species/purple-moor-grass-380.webp 380w, ../assets/img/species/purple-moor-grass-760.webp 760w" sizes="(min-width:700px) 150px, 300px"><img src="../assets/img/species/purple-moor-grass-380.webp" width="380" height="253" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="A dense tussock of narrow green grass leaves in open woodland"></picture><figcaption><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Molinia_caerulea_kz08.jpg">Krzysztof Ziarnek, Kenraiz</a>, <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0">CC BY-SA 4.0</a></figcaption></figure>
        <h2 class="sp-name">Purple moor grass</h2>
        <span class="sp-sci"><em>Molinia caerulea</em> (L.) Moench</span>
        <p class="sp-desc">A deciduous perennial grass, abundant on wet heath, moorland, blanket bog, mire and fen, and more scattered through open birchwood, montane grassland and stony lake margins. It grows on peats and mineral soils that are wet either permanently or for part of the year.</p><p class="sp-cite"><a href="https://plantatlas2020.org/atlas/2cd4p9h.fgn" rel="noopener">BSBI Plant Atlas 2020</a></p>
        <ul class="sp-recs">
          <li><span class="sp-trail">Corrin Loop</span><span class="sp-date">15 August 2026</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li class="sp">
        <figure class="sp-fig"><picture><source type="image/avif" srcset="../assets/img/species/rowan-380.avif 380w, ../assets/img/species/rowan-760.avif 760w" sizes="(min-width:700px) 150px, 300px"><source type="image/webp" srcset="../assets/img/species/rowan-380.webp 380w, ../assets/img/species/rowan-760.webp 760w" sizes="(min-width:700px) 150px, 300px"><img src="../assets/img/species/rowan-380.webp" width="380" height="253" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Pinnate rowan leaves with paired toothed leaflets along a reddish stalk"></picture><figcaption><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rowan_(Rogn)_(Sorbus_aucuparia)_-_Moskenes,_Norway_2023-07-18.jpg">Ryan Hodnett</a>, <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0">CC BY-SA 4.0</a></figcaption></figure>
        <h2 class="sp-name">Rowan</h2>
        <span class="sp-sci"><em>Sorbus aucuparia</em> L.</span>
        <p class="sp-desc">A small to medium tree of open woodland, cliffs and rocky ground, rocky riversides and moorland. It is widely planted, and birds sow it freely on waste ground and along railways. It avoids dry lime-rich or heavy soils and does not thrive in dense shade.</p><p class="sp-cite"><a href="https://plantatlas2020.org/atlas/2cd4p9h.w2c" rel="noopener">BSBI Plant Atlas 2020</a></p>
        <ul class="sp-recs">
          <li><span class="sp-trail">Old Cattle Trail</span><span class="sp-date">9 August 2026</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li class="sp">
        <figure class="sp-fig"><picture><source type="image/avif" srcset="../assets/img/species/sessile-oak-380.avif 380w, ../assets/img/species/sessile-oak-760.avif 760w" sizes="(min-width:700px) 150px, 300px"><source type="image/webp" srcset="../assets/img/species/sessile-oak-380.webp 380w, ../assets/img/species/sessile-oak-760.webp 760w" sizes="(min-width:700px) 150px, 300px"><img src="../assets/img/species/sessile-oak-380.webp" width="380" height="253" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="An ancient oak with a broad low crown of twisting bare branches on a rocky slope"></picture><figcaption><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_Sessile_Oak_(Quercus_petraea),_Falls_of_Cruachan.JPG">Rosser1954</a>, <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0">CC BY-SA 4.0</a></figcaption></figure>
        <h2 class="sp-name">Sessile oak</h2>
        <span class="sp-sci"><em>Quercus petraea</em> (Matt.) Liebl.</span>
        <p class="sp-desc">A long-lived deciduous tree forming high forest or coppice woodland, usually on well-drained, shallow, moderately to strongly acid soils. It is the characteristic species of upland oakwood. It varies greatly in form, and telling it from pedunculate oak in the field is not always clear-cut.</p><p class="sp-cite"><a href="https://plantatlas2020.org/atlas/2cd4p9h.vse" rel="noopener">BSBI Plant Atlas 2020</a></p>
        <ul class="sp-recs">
          <li><span class="sp-trail">Corrin Loop</span><span class="sp-date">15 August 2026</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li class="sp">
        <figure class="sp-fig"><picture><source type="image/avif" srcset="../assets/img/species/tormentil-380.avif 380w, ../assets/img/species/tormentil-760.avif 760w" sizes="(min-width:700px) 150px, 300px"><source type="image/webp" srcset="../assets/img/species/tormentil-380.webp 380w, ../assets/img/species/tormentil-760.webp 760w" sizes="(min-width:700px) 150px, 300px"><img src="../assets/img/species/tormentil-380.webp" width="380" height="253" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Small four-petalled yellow flowers scattered through low green foliage"></picture><figcaption><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Potentilla_erecta_2_TK_2021-07-28.jpg">T. Kebert</a>, <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0">CC BY-SA 4.0</a></figcaption></figure>
        <h2 class="sp-name">Tormentil</h2>
        <span class="sp-sci"><em>Potentilla erecta</em> (L.) Raeusch. <span class="sp-flag">proposed determination</span></span>
        <p class="sp-desc">A perennial herb of more or less acid soils, found in grassland from the lowlands to the mountains, in hay and fen meadows, on moorland and heath, across blanket and raised mire, and along wood borders and hedgebanks. Stock find it unpalatable, and it does not tolerate much shade.</p><p class="sp-cite"><a href="https://plantatlas2020.org/atlas/2cd4p9h.5v7" rel="noopener">BSBI Plant Atlas 2020</a></p>
        <ul class="sp-recs">
          <li><span class="sp-trail">Old Cattle Trail</span><span class="sp-date">9 August 2026</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li class="sp">
        <figure class="sp-fig"><picture><source type="image/avif" srcset="../assets/img/species/water-figwort-380.avif 380w, ../assets/img/species/water-figwort-760.avif 760w" sizes="(min-width:700px) 150px, 300px"><source type="image/webp" srcset="../assets/img/species/water-figwort-380.webp 380w, ../assets/img/species/water-figwort-760.webp 760w" sizes="(min-width:700px) 150px, 300px"><img src="../assets/img/species/water-figwort-380.webp" width="380" height="253" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Tall flowering stems of water figwort at the edge of still water"></picture><figcaption><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scrophularia_auriculata_07.2025_(1).jpg">DidierFy</a>, <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0">CC BY-SA 4.0</a></figcaption></figure>
        <h2 class="sp-name">Water figwort</h2>
        <span class="sp-sci"><em>Scrophularia auriculata</em> L.</span>
        <p class="sp-desc">A partly winter-green perennial herb of water margins: ponds, pits, lakes, rivers, streams and canals, and ditches, marshes, wet meadows, fens and wet woodland. It keeps much closer to water than common figwort, with which it is sometimes confused. Usually a lowland plant.</p><p class="sp-cite"><a href="https://plantatlas2020.org/atlas/2cd4p9h.w1g" rel="noopener">BSBI Plant Atlas 2020</a></p>
        <ul class="sp-recs">
          <li><span class="sp-trail">Corrin Loop</span><span class="sp-date">15 August 2026</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
    </ul>
